Random forests (rf.R, mcrf.R, psockrf.R, mpirf.R)

A decision tree predicts by asking a series of yes/no questions about the
features (is x1 > 0.3? is x2 < -1?) until it reaches a leaf with an answer.
One deep tree fits its training data too closely, so a random forest grows
many trees, each on a random resample of the rows and choosing from a random
subset of the features at every split. The forest predicts by majority vote
of its trees, which is far more accurate than any single tree.

Why it parallelizes well: every tree is grown independently, so different
workers can grow different trees and the results are simply pooled.
